ut, finishing 19th."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/150154"} "Mattia Furlani"] ", a high jump / long jump combo jumper who had won both events at the 2022 European U-18 Championships, led the final in round one, jumping 8.34 metres, with Tentoglou in second at 8.27. Round two ultimately decided the podium as Tentoglou cleared 8.48 metres to defend his gold medal, and Pinnock moved into second with 8.36. Furlani’s 8.25 was his second best mark of the evening, and he would win bronze."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Tentoglou became only the second man to defend Olympic long jump gold, after " [:a {:href "/athletes/78692"} "Carl Lewis"] ", who uniquely won four consecutive golds from 1984-96, although " [:a {:href "/athletes/78917"} "Meyer Prinstein"] " (USA) also won gold in 1904 and 1906."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Qualifying"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "791"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/134267"} "Miltiadis Tentoglou"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GRE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GRE.png"}] "GRE"]] [:td {} "8.32 (1)"] [:td {} "8.48 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "909"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/150202"} "Wayne Pinnock"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/JAM"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/JAM.png"}] "JAM"]] [:td {} "7.96 (7)"] [:td {} "8.36 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "854"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/150154"} "Mattia Furlani"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:st